 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Increases training time per iteration due to the additional rendering pass for novel view rays [S1].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storing gradients for the novel view rays, increasing memory usage [S1].
-*   **Performance:** Reduces overfitting and enhances geometry accuracy compared to training without novel view supervision [S1]. Geometric adaptation significantly reduces floaters [S1].
-*   **Strengths:** Improves generalization to diverse scenes and reduces local minima convergence [S1].
-*   **Limitations:** Requires careful selection of novel view poses to ensure effective coverage [S1].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Increases training time due to the additional rendering pass for novel views [S1].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** No significant additional memory overhead beyond storing the novel view rays and intermediate rendering buffers [S1].
+*   **Performance:** Reduces overfitting and enhances geometry accuracy compared to training without novel view supervision [S1]. It significantly reduces floaters and improves cross-scale geometric adaptation [S1].
+*   **Strengths:** Improves rendering quality and geometric consistency in few-shot scenarios [S1].
+*   **Limitations:** Requires careful balancing of the novel view loss weight to avoid destabilizing training [S1].
 
 ## Background
-Novel View Regularization was introduced in the context of FrugalNeRF, a method for fast convergence in extreme few-shot novel view synthesis without learned priors [S1]. It addresses the challenge of reconstructing high-quality geometry from very few input views by leveraging virtual viewpoints to provide additional constraints during optimization [S1].
+Novel View Regularization is used in the context of FrugalNeRF, a method for fast convergence in extreme few-shot novel view synthesis [S1]. It addresses the challenge of reconstructing 3D scenes from very limited input views by leveraging geometric priors through novel view supervision [S1].
 
